{
  "abstract": "A dimer cycle of the Syracuse map is a cycle whose valuation word is a product of M circuits (1,2)^u (2)^v; these are the positive cycles of the map H of Yolcu, Aaronson and Heule. collatz_71 showed that growing floors for the least elements of trough windows would exclude all dimer cycles: if every two-sided trough centre of depth L satisfies Q >= 1,047,039 * 3.59^(L-1), or every forward trough satisfies Q >= 14,847 * 1.90^(L-1), there is none. Call a window eligible if each of its points other than the centre, as an affine function of the centre, has slope greater than 1. Non-eligible windows hold only for bounded centres, and they carry the small centres of the maps 3x+k for which the floors fail. We prove that the floors are needed only on eligible windows, with bases 3.29 (two-sided) and 1.82 (forward). The proof splits the cycle at the first circuit where the forward slope from the minimum drops below 1, and bounds the minimum by the cycle equation on that prefix, whose two ends both lie near the minimum. The input is a circuit-product bound 0 < S ln 2 - R ln 3 < 6M/(x_min - 1), due to lyapunov_weaver, which lowers the bases from 3.59 and 1.90 to 3.29 and 1.82 for all windows as well. Without hypothesis, no dimer cycle has M <= 409 circuits. A backward residue search with the dimer rules shows that no dimer cycle has least element below 2^90, and hence none has M <= 983 circuits. All bounds are certified in interval arithmetic. The floors themselves remain unproved.",
